(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-consumer"]={"plain":{"bills":{"consumer":{"slug":"consumer","title":"The Honest Price Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title.","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the \"Honest Price Act.\""},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings.","part":null,"body":"Congress finds as follows:\n\n1. A term of a consumer agreement that is not seen at the time of decision is not priced by competition. A seller that reveals a mandatory charge only after the consumer has invested effort in the purchase, or that relies on the consumer's inertia to continue a subscription, or that places the forum for any later dispute in a document essentially no consumer reads, gains from each of those practices at the expense of consumers who cannot compare on the concealed term. A competitor that reveals the term loses the advantage and gains no customers, so competition among sellers does not correct the practice and in ordinary conditions sustains it. Correcting these practices is the precondition for a competitive market in consumer goods and services, not a departure from one.\n2. In a randomized field experiment on a national ticket marketplace, revealing mandatory fees only at checkout raised revenue per user by about 20 percent; consumers shown the fees late were almost 45 percent less likely to purchase once they reached checkout, but were more likely to purchase overall and spent more in total. Consumer inertia under automatic renewal roughly doubles a subscription seller's revenue on average. In a study of software purchasers, one or two of every thousand opened the license agreement governing their purchase.\n3. The study of consumer arbitration that Congress commissioned in section 1028 of the Consumer Financial Protection Act of 2010 found that class settlements paid at least $220 million a year to an average of 6.8 million consumers, while about 600 consumer arbitrations a year yielded under $400,000 in relief to consumers, and companies collected $2.8 million from consumers in the same forum. In suits against credit card issuers whose agreements contained an arbitration clause, the issuers moved to compel arbitration in 65 percent of class actions and 5.7 percent of individual suits; more than 90 percent of arbitration agreements forbade class arbitration; and three of four consumers surveyed did not know whether their agreement contained an arbitration clause. A predispute arbitration agreement in a consumer contract operates as a bar to collective relief that no consumer selected.\n4. When four large card issuers ceased using arbitration clauses for three and a half years under a litigation settlement, the Bureau of Consumer Financial Protection found no statistically significant change in the total cost of credit or in the credit offered, relative to issuers that retained the clauses. That finding is a null result and not a demonstration that no effect occurred. Congress enacted the same election for sexual-harassment and sexual-assault disputes in chapter 4 of title 9, United States Code, in 2022, and for servicemember credit in 10 U.S.C. \u00a7 987 in 2007, and credit continued to be extended in both markets.\n5. A small share of deposit accounts pays most overdraft and nonsufficient-funds fees. In data the Bureau published in 2017, 9.1 percent of accounts paid 78.7 percent of all such fees, and 4.9 percent paid 63.3 percent. Since that measurement many large institutions have reduced or eliminated those fees. The concentration finding describes a structural feature of contingent charges: an average stated alone understates what the accounts that bear the charges actually pay.\n6. Dollar-denominated cost information, supplied in a form the consumer can compare without searching for the comparison, moves consumer choice more than percentage-denominated information or information the consumer must assemble. Published comparative information also changes the conduct of the firms that must publish it, and that effect is measured to be larger and more consistent than the effect on the consumers who receive it. This Act is drafted for both channels and does not depend on either alone.\n7. Medical collections predict repayment less accurately than other collections, and the principal credit-scoring models and the nationwide consumer reporting agencies have already reduced or excluded them for that reason. Excluding medical debt information from consumer reports makes the credit market more accurate. It relieves no debt.\n8. Every regulatory instrument addressing the practices in this Act has been withdrawn or set aside: the Bureau's 2017 arbitration rule and 2024 overdraft rule by joint resolution under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, which bars their reissuance in substantially the same form absent a later Act of Congress; the Federal Trade Commission's 2024 negative option rule and the Bureau's 2025 medical debt rule by judicial vacatur. The administrative records supporting each remain. Only a statute is durable against those routes, and this Act enacts each rule in its own operative text.\n9. Nothing in this Act sets, limits, or reviews any price, rate, or fee; bans any product; or requires any seller to offer any product. Where a State has enacted greater protection, this Act is a floor and not a ceiling. Each provision of this Act operates without any action by any agency, and each is independently severable.\n10. A consumer cannot verify a score, or the amounts by which its inputs moved it, without the formula that produced it, and the accuracy and comparability of scoring models can be tested only by persons other than the consumer, using the formula. Publication to the public is the means by which that testing occurs."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions.","part":null,"body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a)** \"Consumer\" means a natural person who enters, or is offered, a consumer agreement.\n\n**(b)** \"Consumer agreement\" means an agreement, whether written, electronic, oral, or implied from conduct, for goods, services, credit, a deposit or transaction account, or the use of property, obtained primarily for personal, family, or household purposes, including such an agreement entered through an agent, marketplace, or platform.\n\n**(c)** \"Covered person\" means a person that, in the ordinary course of business, enters consumer agreements, or offers, displays, or quotes to consumers the price of goods or services that are the subject of a consumer agreement, including a person acting as a marketplace, platform, or agent for another; and includes a financial institution and a creditor.\n\n**(d)** \"Affiliate\" means a person that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with a covered person, and a person that furnishes, under agreement with a covered person, a product or service offered in connection with the covered person's consumer agreements.\n\n**(e)** \"Consumer dispute\" means a dispute between a consumer and a covered person, or its affiliate, arising from or relating to a consumer agreement, whatever the source of law under which a claim is asserted.\n\n**(f)** \"Charge\" means any amount a consumer pays or becomes obligated to pay to a covered person or an affiliate in connection with a consumer agreement, regardless of its name, form, or characterization.\n\n**(g)** \"Government-imposed charge\" means a tax, fee, or assessment that a government imposes on the consumer or on the transaction, that the covered person collects for that government, and that the covered person remits to it in full. A charge whose amount the covered person sets, or any part of which the covered person retains, is not a government-imposed charge, whatever it is called.\n\n**(h)** \"Mandatory charge\" means a charge a consumer cannot avoid while obtaining the goods or services offered, regardless of its name, form, or characterization, and whether or not the consumer may reduce it. A charge is not made avoidable by a default the consumer must act to remove.\n\n**(i)** \"Optional addition\" means a good, service, or feature that the consumer selects by an affirmative act, that is not preselected, and without which the consumer may obtain the goods or services offered on the terms displayed, at the same time and place and by the same means; an addition whose only alternative requires the consumer to obtain the goods or services at a different time or place, by a materially different means, or on different terms is a mandatory charge.\n\n**(j)** \"Displayed or quoted\" includes any advertisement, listing, offer, search result, price comparison, oral or written quotation, and periodic billing statement, in any medium.\n\n**(k)** \"Predispute\" describes an agreement or term entered before the conduct giving rise to the consumer dispute occurred, whenever a claim is asserted.\n\n**(l)** \"Joint-action waiver\" means an agreement or term that would prohibit, or waive the right of, a party to a consumer dispute to participate in a joint, class, collective, or representative proceeding concerning it.\n\n**(m)** \"Account\" means the relationship under a consumer agreement to which charges are posted, and includes a subscription, a line of service, and a deposit, transaction, or credit account.\n\n**(n)** \"Bureau\" means the Bureau of Consumer Financial Protection, and \"Commission\" means the Federal Trade Commission. If the Bureau ceases to exist, or ceases to perform a function this Act assigns to it, the Commission performs the function, and a reference to the Bureau is a reference to the Commission for that purpose.\n\n**(o)** \"Court of record\" means a court of the United States or a court of a State, other than a small-claims court or division or a proceeding under rules for small claims.\n\n**(p)** \"State\" includes the District of Columbia and each territory; Tribal governments join every provision of this Act that preserves, funds, protects, empowers, or recognizes, and this Act binds a Tribal government only as to a function voluntarily assumed.\n\n**(q)** \"Consumer reporting agency,\" \"consumer report,\" \"creditor,\" \"credit,\" \"finance charge,\" and \"medical information\" have the meanings given them in the Fair Credit Reporting Act and the Truth in Lending Act, as amended by this Act, except as Title IV-A provides; and \"financial institution\" has the meaning given it in section 903 of the Electronic Fund Transfer Act (15 U.S.C. 1693a)."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. Predispute arbitration agreements and joint-action waivers.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISPUTES: THE CONSUMER'S ELECTION","body":"**(a)** Notwithstanding chapter 1 of title 9, United States Code, at the election of the consumer, or of the named representative of a class, collective, or representative action, no predispute arbitration agreement and no predispute joint-action waiver is valid or enforceable with respect to a case relating to a consumer dispute, and \"case\" means the entire civil proceeding, not the claim alone. The election may be made after the dispute arises, and a term purporting to require the election earlier, or to waive it, is void.\n\n**(b)** A court shall not compel arbitration of any part of such a case unless it first determines that this section does not apply, notwithstanding any delegation of that determination to an arbitrator and notwithstanding any incorporation of arbitral rules.\n\n**(c)** The election is available where the claim is pleaded nonfrivolously, and no more is required to invoke it.\n\n**(d)** Where any provision of an agreement is unenforceable under this section, the arbitration agreement or joint-action waiver is unenforceable in its entirety as to that case, notwithstanding any severability term in it. The remainder of the consumer agreement is unaffected.\n\n**(e)** This section applies to an agreement entered before, on, or after the date of enactment of this Act, and to a consumer dispute arising on or after that date, and to a consumer dispute arising before that date in which no arbitration had been commenced before that date.\n\n**(f)** Nothing in this section limits the operation of section 1 or section 402 of title 9, United States Code, and the protections are cumulative.\n\n**(g)** A covered person violates this Act by invoking, or seeking to compel arbitration or dismissal under, an agreement or term unenforceable under this section after the consumer has made the election.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 DISPUTES: THE CONSUMER'S ELECTION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Post-dispute arbitration agreements.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISPUTES: THE CONSUMER'S ELECTION","body":"**(a)** An agreement to arbitrate a consumer dispute, or to waive joint action concerning it, entered after the dispute arises, is valid only if knowing and voluntary. An agreement is knowing and voluntary only if each of the following is satisfied:\n\n**(1)** it is a document separate from any settlement, refund, credit, adjustment, goodwill offer, or other resolution of the dispute, and is signed separately;\n\n**(2)** it is written in a manner calculated to be understood by the consumer, and states in plain language that the consumer may instead proceed in court, including in a class or representative action where available, and that the choice is the consumer's;\n\n**(3)** the consumer is given at least the period set in the schedule to consider it before signing, and may revoke it within the period set in the schedule after signing, and the agreement states both periods;\n\n**(4)** no relief the consumer is otherwise owed, and no refund, credit, or adjustment the covered person offers, is conditioned on the consumer's signing it, and the covered person does not withdraw or reduce an offer because the consumer declines; and\n\n**(5)** where the arbitration provider named in it publishes aggregate statistics of the outcomes of consumer arbitrations it administers, the agreement states where those statistics are published.\n\n**(b)** A settlement, release, or other resolution of a consumer dispute conditioned on an agreement that does not satisfy subsection (a) is voidable at the consumer's election within the revocation period set in the schedule, and the consumer's election does not forfeit any refund, credit, adjustment, or other relief the resolution provided. A covered person that seeks to recover relief so provided violates this Act.\n\n**(c)** The party asserting that an agreement is knowing and voluntary bears the burden of establishing it.\n\n**(d)** Nothing in this section conditions the availability of arbitration chosen by both parties after a dispute arises, requires any report of its use, or limits any right under a post-dispute agreement that satisfies subsection (a)."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Construction.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISPUTES: THE CONSUMER'S ELECTION","body":"**(a)** This title governs the forum in which a consumer dispute is heard. It does not create, enlarge, limit, or determine any claim or defense in the dispute, and no term of the bargain other than the forum term is affected by it.\n\n**(b)** A consumer's election under section 1-1 as to one dispute is not an election as to any other, and a consumer who has arbitrated one dispute under a valid post-dispute agreement retains the election as to every other."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Policies of insurance.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISPUTES: THE CONSUMER'S ELECTION","body":"**(a)** This title and Title V relate specifically to the business of insurance as applied to a consumer agreement that is a policy of insurance, and apply to such a policy notwithstanding section 2(b) of the Act of March 9, 1945 (15 U.S.C. 1012(b)).\n\n**(b)** Whether or not subsection (a) is given effect, this title and Title V apply to a consumer agreement that is a policy of insurance to the extent the law of the State does not govern the validity or enforceability of a term of the kind those titles address.\n\n**(c)** If subsection (a) is held invalid or inapplicable as to any State law, this title and Title V do not invalidate, impair, or supersede that State law to the extent it regulates the business of insurance, and subsection (b) states their application.\n\n**(d)** The invalidity of subsection (a) does not affect subsection (b), subsection (c), or any other provision of this Act, and no court shall treat subsection (a) and subsection (b) as an integrated scheme."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. The price displayed is the total price.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 THE WHOLE PRICE","body":"**(a)** Whenever a covered person displays or quotes a price for goods or services offered under a consumer agreement, the price displayed or quoted is the total amount the consumer will pay for those goods or services, including every mandatory charge.\n\n**(b)** The total price may exclude only:\n\n**(1)** a government-imposed charge, if the display or quotation states that such charges are excluded and, where the amount is determinable before the consumer commits, states the amount; and\n\n**(2)** the price of an optional addition, if the display or quotation states the price of the addition beside it.\n\nNo other charge may be excluded from the total price, and the omission of any other exclusion is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(c)** A charge is a mandatory charge for purposes of this section regardless of whether it is described as a fee, surcharge, service charge, gratuity, convenience charge, processing charge, facility charge, delivery charge, or any other name; whether it is imposed by the covered person, an affiliate, or a person on whose behalf the covered person displays the price; and whether it is stated as a fixed amount or a percentage. A mandatory service charge or gratuity added to the price of food or beverage service is a mandatory charge.\n\n**(d)** A charge that varies with the consumer's use, or that is contingent on an event after the agreement is entered, is not a mandatory charge for purposes of subsection (a) if the total price displayed states every amount that is fixed, and the variable or contingent charges are stated as Title II-A requires. A charge described as variable or contingent that a consumer cannot in practice avoid is a mandatory charge.\n\n**(e)** Where a price is displayed by a marketplace, platform, search service, or agent, this section applies to that person as to the price it displays, and to the seller as to the price it furnishes for display.\n\n**(f)** A periodic billing statement satisfies this section if the total amount payable for the period is stated before and at least as prominently as any component of it.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 THE WHOLE PRICE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Presentation.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 THE WHOLE PRICE","body":"**(a)** The total price under section 2-1 shall be displayed at least as prominently as any component of it, any comparison price, and any price stated for a shorter period or a smaller unit than the one the consumer will purchase. Prominence is measured by type size, contrast, placement, and, in a medium that displays for a limited time, duration.\n\n**(b)** This section is separately severable from section 2-1. The invalidity of any part of this section does not affect section 2-1 or any other provision of this Act, and a court shall not treat this section and section 2-1 as an integrated scheme."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Scope.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 THE WHOLE PRICE","body":"**(a)** This title applies to every consumer agreement and every covered person, including a financial institution, a creditor, a provider of broadband, wireless, video, or other communications service, a provider of lodging or transportation, a seller or reseller of tickets, a provider of food or beverage service, a provider of delivery or platform services, and a lessor of goods or property. No sector is exempt from this title except as this Act states, and the omission of an exemption is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(b)** This title is cumulative of every other Federal requirement that a price be stated in a particular manner, including a requirement under section 41712 of title 49, United States Code, and a rule of the Commission, and nothing in this title limits any such requirement.\n\n**(c)** A State, local, or Tribal law requiring that a displayed price include a charge, or regulating the manner in which a price is displayed, is not preempted by this title to the extent it affords a consumer greater protection.\n\n**(d)** For an extension of credit or a deposit or transaction account, the price displayed under section 2-1 is the amount, rate, and yield the Truth in Lending Act or the Truth in Savings Act requires to be stated for the advertisement or offer, together with every mandatory charge not included in those figures and the figures Title II-A requires; the form of the statement is governed by those Acts, and section 2-2 applies to the total so stated."},{"anchor":"sec-2a-1","heading":"\u00a7 2A-1. The figures stated at decision time.","part":"TITLE II-A \u2014 THE AVERAGE COST UNDER CONTRACT","body":"**(a)** This title applies to a consumer agreement under which the total amount payable during the standard period is not fixed at the time the agreement is entered, including an agreement carrying a recurring charge, a usage-based charge, a charge contingent on an event after the agreement is entered, or a single charge deferred to a later date.\n\n**(b)** Whenever a covered person displays or quotes the price of, advertises, or offers such an agreement, and in the disclosure furnished when the account is opened, the covered person states, for the functional product class to which the agreement belongs, the two figures computed under section 2A-2 for the most recent standard period:\n\n**(1)** the average total amount paid by an account during the standard period; and\n\n**(2)** the amount that one in ten accounts paid more than during the standard period.\n\n**(c)** The figures are stated in dollars, in the words \"the average customer paid $[amount] over twelve months\" and \"one in ten customers paid more than $[amount],\" or words of equivalent meaning, with the period identified, and are the same figures as those filed under section 2A-3 for that class and period.\n\n**(d)** A covered person that has no filed figures for a class, because it has not completed a standard period in that class, states in their place the class-wide figures the Bureau publishes under section 2A-3(d), identified as the class-wide figures, and if none are published, states that no figure is available for the class.\n\n**(e)** Where the number of accounts in a class is fewer than the number set in the schedule, the covered person states the number of accounts beside the figures.\n\n**(f)** Nothing in this title prevents a covered person from stating, beside the figures, any accurate additional information about its charges, provided the figures are stated first and not less prominently.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II-A \u2014 THE AVERAGE COST UNDER CONTRACT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2a-2","heading":"\u00a7 2A-2. Method of computation.","part":"TITLE II-A \u2014 THE AVERAGE COST UNDER CONTRACT","body":"**(a) The enterprise.** The figures are computed for the covered person and every affiliate as a single enterprise. A charge paid to an affiliate in connection with an account is a charge on that account.\n\n**(b) The class.** The figures are computed for each functional product class set in the schedule. A class is defined by the function the agreement performs for the consumer and not by the covered person's product names, tiers, brands, or channels. Every account that performs the function belongs to the class, whatever the covered person calls it.\n\n**(c) The accounts.** The figures are computed over every account in the class that was open at any time during the standard period, including an account opened or closed during it, on which the consumer initiated at least one transaction during the period or which was charged during the period; an account the covered person opened without a request by the consumer and on which the consumer initiated no transaction is not counted.\n\n**(d) The amount paid.** The amount paid by an account is every charge the consumer paid during the standard period in connection with the account, to the covered person or any affiliate, including every fee, interest, finance charge, membership or service charge, penalty, and third-party charge collected by the covered person, and excluding only:\n\n**(1)** a government-imposed charge;\n\n**(2)** principal repaid on an extension of credit; and\n\n**(3)** the price of a discrete good or service the consumer separately and affirmatively selected, if that price was displayed under Title II when selected.\n\n**(e) The standard period.** The standard period is the period set in the schedule, and the figures for each period are computed within the period set in the schedule after it ends.\n\n**(f) The figures.** The average is the arithmetic mean of the amounts paid by the accounts under subsection (c). The one-in-ten figure is the amount exceeded by the amounts paid by the ten percent of those accounts that paid the most.\n\n**(g) No discretion.** The covered person exercises no discretion in the computation. No cohort, exclusion, assumption, or adjustment other than those this section states may be applied, and no regulation may add or permit one."},{"anchor":"sec-2a-3","heading":"\u00a7 2A-3. Filing and publication.","part":"TITLE II-A \u2014 THE AVERAGE COST UNDER CONTRACT","body":"**(a)** Within the period set in the schedule after each standard period ends, each covered person files with the Bureau, for each class: the two figures; the number of accounts under section 2A-2(c); the number of accounts that paid no charge; the number of applications for an account in the class received and the number denied during the period; and the number of accounts in the class closed or restricted by the covered person during the period other than at the consumer's request.\n\n**(b)** Within the period set in the schedule after the filing deadline, the Bureau publishes every filing, in one place, in a single table for each class in which the covered persons appear beside one another with their figures and the rates computed from the counts in subsection (a), in a standardized, machine-readable, and bulk-downloadable form, together with the class-wide figures under subsection (d).\n\n**(c)** Publication is a ministerial duty. The Bureau has no discretion to withhold, delay, annotate, adjust, or rank a filing, and publishes every filing as filed. A covered person that misstates a filing is liable under Title VII; the Bureau is not.\n\n**(d)** The Bureau computes and publishes, for each class and period, the class-wide figures under section 2A-2(f) treating every account filed for the class as a single population, and identifies in the publication, by citation, each provision of this Act and of any other Federal law applying to the class that took effect during the period; a publication that omits a provision of this Act that took effect during the period does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(e)** Where the Bureau has not published within the period set in the schedule, any covered person that has filed and any consumer may bring an action in a district court of the United States for an order compelling publication, and the court shall enter the order on proof of the filing and the lapse of the period, without regard to any other question. The duty to publish and the appropriation under section 8-4 continue during any lapse in appropriations.\n\n**(f)** The duty of a covered person under section 2A-1 does not depend on publication under this section, and is not suspended, tolled, or excused by any failure of the Bureau."},{"anchor":"sec-2a-4","heading":"\u00a7 2A-4. Improvement by exclusion.","part":"TITLE II-A \u2014 THE AVERAGE COST UNDER CONTRACT","body":"**(a)** A covered person shall not close, restrict, or decline to open an account in a class on the ground of the charges under this title the account has paid or would be expected to pay, and shall not apply a criterion for closure, restriction, or denial that uses that amount or its components. Nothing in this subsection limits closure, restriction, or denial on the ground of an amount owed and unpaid after the period set in the schedule, of fraud, or of a requirement of other Federal law; but the covered person records the ground at the time of the closure, restriction, or denial, and a ground not so recorded may not be asserted.\n\n**(b)** A denial, closure, or restriction of an account that is credit under Title IV shall be treated as an adverse action for purposes of section 701(d) of the Equal Credit Opportunity Act, and the duties of that subsection apply to it.\n\n**(c)** The rates computed from the counts filed under section 2A-3(a) are published beside the figures under section 2A-3(b), for the same class and period, and a covered person that states its figures under section 2A-1 in advertising states those rates beside them in each advertising medium set in the schedule."},{"anchor":"sec-2a-5","heading":"\u00a7 2A-5. Disclosures displaced.","part":"TITLE II-A \u2014 THE AVERAGE COST UNDER CONTRACT","body":"**(a)** Section 263(a)(5) of the Truth in Savings Act (12 U.S.C. 4302(a)(5)) is amended to read as follows, with the struck text marked and new language marked as inserted:\n\n> ~~(5) A statement that regular fees or other conditions could reduce the yield.~~\n>\n> **(5) The figures stated under section 2A-1 of the Honest Price Act for the class to which the account belongs.**\n\n**(b)** Section 60504(b)(1) of the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act (47 U.S.C. 1753(b)(1)) is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**The label shall also state the figures under section 2A-1 of the Honest Price Act for the class to which the plan belongs, and no separate statement of typical, estimated, or average cost is required on the label.**\"\n\n**(c)** Where any other Federal law or regulation requires a covered person to state, in an advertisement or offer, a typical, estimated, expected, or average cost of an account or agreement to which this title applies, the figures stated under section 2A-1 satisfy that requirement, and no separate statement is required. Nothing in this section alters a requirement to itemize the fees, charges, or terms of an account, including under section 264 of the Truth in Savings Act (12 U.S.C. 4303)."},{"anchor":"sec-2a-6","heading":"\u00a7 2A-6. Use of the published figures.","part":"TITLE II-A \u2014 THE AVERAGE COST UNDER CONTRACT","body":"**(a)** The figures and rates published under section 2A-3 are the official figures for the class and period, and any person may cite them as such.\n\n**(b)** A covered person may state, in advertising or otherwise, its own published figures and rates and those of any other covered person for the same class and period. A statement that reproduces the published figures accurately, identifies the class and period, and does not attribute to the figures a meaning the publication does not carry, is not false, misleading, deceptive, or unfair under any Federal or State law by reason of the comparison, and no covered person has a claim against another by reason of it.\n\n**(c)** No provision of this title, and no figure published under it, establishes, limits, or approves the amount of any charge, and a published figure is not a benchmark, standard, or rate for any purpose."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Amendment of section 4 of the Restore Online Shoppers' Confidence Act.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 EXIT AS EASY AS ENTRANCE","body":"Section 4 of the Restore Online Shoppers' Confidence Act (15 U.S.C. 8403) is amended to read as follows, with new language marked as inserted and struck language marked:\n\n> **SEC. 4. NEGATIVE OPTION MARKETING** ~~ON THE INTERNET~~**.**\n>\n> **(a) Requirements.** It shall be unlawful for any person to charge or attempt to charge any consumer for any goods or services sold ~~in a transaction effected on the Internet~~ **in any transaction, in any channel or medium,** through a negative option feature ~~(as defined in the Federal Trade Commission's Telemarketing Sales Rule in part 310 of title 16, Code of Federal Regulations)~~ **(as defined in subsection (e))**, unless the person\u2014\n>\n> (1) provides text that clearly and conspicuously discloses all material terms of the transaction**, including the amount and frequency of each recurring charge, the date of the first charge following any free, trial, or introductory period, and the means by which the consumer may cancel,** before obtaining the consumer's billing information;\n>\n> (2) obtains a consumer's express informed consent before charging the consumer's credit card, debit card, bank account, or other financial account for products or services through such transaction; ~~and~~\n>\n> (3) provides simple mechanisms for a consumer to stop recurring charges from being placed on the consumer's credit card, debit card, bank account, or other financial account**, and a mechanism is simple only if it satisfies subsection (b);**\n>\n> **(4) before the first charge following a free, trial, or introductory period, and not less than the period set in the schedule to the Honest Price Act before it, notifies the consumer of the amount and date of the charge and the means of cancellation; and**\n>\n> **(5) before imposing any charge following a change described in subsection (c), obtains the consumer's affirmative consent to continue, in the manner subsection (d) requires.**\n>\n> **(b) Cancellation.** A mechanism to stop recurring charges is simple only if\u2014\n>\n> **(1) the consumer may cancel by the same means the consumer used to enroll, and, where the person accepts enrollment or payment by any online means, by an online means;**\n>\n> **(2) cancellation requires no more steps, time, or information than enrollment required;**\n>\n> **(3) no offer, question, or step prohibited by section 6-1 of the Honest Price Act is interposed;**\n>\n> **(4) the cancellation takes effect on receipt, and the person confirms it to the consumer in writing within the period set in the schedule to that Act; and**\n>\n> **(5) no charge is imposed after cancellation, other than for a period for which the consumer has already been charged and which continues to the end of that period.**\n>\n> **(c) A change in the deal.** A change described in this subsection is any of the following:\n>\n> **(1) the amount the consumer will be charged for a period exceeds the amount charged for the preceding period of equal length, or the amount disclosed under subsection (a)(1), other than by reason of use the consumer initiated;**\n>\n> **(2) a free, trial, promotional, or introductory price or rate expires and a higher price or rate takes effect;**\n>\n> **(3) the goods or services provided for the recurring charge are reduced, or a feature disclosed under subsection (a)(1) is withdrawn, without a reduction of the charge; or**\n>\n> **(4) the consumer is transferred, migrated, or reassigned to a differently named, priced, or structured plan, agreement, or product, and the amount payable for a period increases.**\n>\n> **A change is described in this subsection whatever the person calls it, and whether or not it is described as a price change.**\n>\n> **(d) Consent to continue.** Consent to continue is obtained only by an affirmative act of the consumer, after a notice given not less than the period set in the schedule to the Honest Price Act before the change takes effect, stating the amount the consumer paid for the preceding period of equal length, the amount the consumer will be charged for the coming period, and the means of cancellation. Silence, continued use, or failure to cancel is not consent. Where the consumer does not consent, the person shall not impose the changed amount, and may end the agreement at the end of the period for which the consumer has already been charged. No consent is required for a period in which no change described in subsection (c) occurs, and nothing in this section requires a consumer to renew consent to an agreement whose terms are unchanged.**\n>\n> **(e) Negative option feature.** In this section, \"negative option feature\" means, in an offer or agreement to sell or provide any goods or services, a provision under which the consumer's silence or failure to take an affirmative action to reject goods or services or to cancel the agreement is interpreted by the seller as acceptance of the offer, and includes a provision under which an agreement continues, renews, or converts from a free or reduced price to a higher one unless the consumer acts.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 EXIT AS EASY AS ENTRANCE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Relation to this Act and to other law.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 EXIT AS EASY AS ENTRANCE","body":"**(a)** A violation of section 4 of the Restore Online Shoppers' Confidence Act, as amended by section 3-1, is a violation of this Act, and Title VII applies to it in addition to sections 5 and 6 of that Act.\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this title preempts a State, local, or Tribal law governing automatic renewal, continuous service, or negative option agreements that affords a consumer greater protection.\n\n**(c)** A regulation of the Commission under section 18 of the Federal Trade Commission Act governing negative option features remains in effect to the extent it affords a consumer greater protection than section 4 of the Restore Online Shoppers' Confidence Act as amended, and no such regulation may narrow that section."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Amendment of the Truth in Lending Act.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 OVERDRAFT IS CREDIT","body":"**(a)** Section 106(a) of the Truth in Lending Act (15 U.S.C. 1605(a)) is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**(7) A charge imposed by a financial institution for paying an item, transaction, or transfer that overdraws a deposit or transaction account, or imposed by reason of such an overdraft in any form, whether per item, per day, per period, or by tier of account, and whatever its name, whether or not the payment of such items and the imposition of the charge were previously agreed upon in writing, other than a charge under a courtesy program described in section 4-2 of the Honest Price Act, and the second sentence of this subsection does not apply to such a charge.**\"\n\n**(b)** A financial institution that regularly imposes a charge described in section 106(a)(7) of the Truth in Lending Act, as added by subsection (a), is a creditor, and the payment of an item, transaction, or transfer that overdraws an account is an extension of credit under an open-end consumer credit plan, for purposes of that Act.\n\n**(c)** Any provision of a regulation, interpretation, or commentary under the Truth in Lending Act that excludes a charge described in section 106(a)(7) of that Act from the finance charge, or that excludes the payment of an overdraft from credit, has no effect, and no regulation may exclude either.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 OVERDRAFT IS CREDIT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Courtesy programs.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 OVERDRAFT IS CREDIT","body":"**(a)** A courtesy program is a program under which a financial institution pays items, transactions, or transfers that overdraw an account and whose written terms limit, for every account, the charges for doing so to no more than the number set in the schedule in any twelve-month period and to no more than the aggregate amount set in the schedule in any twelve-month period, and under which no interest or periodic charge accrues on an overdrawn balance.\n\n**(b)** A program whose terms do not so limit the charges is not a courtesy program, whatever it is called, and a charge under it is a finance charge."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Consent.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 OVERDRAFT IS CREDIT","body":"**(a)** A financial institution shall not impose a charge for paying an item, transaction, or transfer that overdraws a consumer's account, of any type, unless the institution:\n\n**(1)** has provided the consumer a notice, segregated from all other information, describing the service, stating that the consumer may decline it and that declining it does not affect any other term of the account, and stating the figures under section 2A-1 for the class to which the account belongs, or the statement section 2A-1(d) provides where none are filed;\n\n**(2)** has obtained the consumer's affirmative consent to the service after that notice, by an act that is not a default and that is not a condition of opening or keeping the account;\n\n**(3)** has confirmed the consent to the consumer in writing, with a statement of the right to revoke it at any time by the same means; and\n\n**(4)** provides to a consumer who has not consented, or who has revoked consent, the same account terms, conditions, and features it provides to a consumer who has consented, other than the service itself.\n\n**(b)** A financial institution shall not decline to pay any item, transaction, or transfer, or condition its payment, on the ground that the consumer has not consented to the service for a different type of transaction.\n\n**(c)** For an account open on the date this section takes effect, the institution shall not impose a charge described in subsection (a) after the period set in the schedule following that date unless it has complied with subsection (a) as to that account.\n\n**(d)** This section applies to every type of item, transaction, and transfer, and is cumulative of section 1005.17 of title 12, Code of Federal Regulations, which continues in effect."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Disclosure.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 OVERDRAFT IS CREDIT","body":"**(a)** For overdraft credit under section 4-1(b), the disclosures required of a creditor under an open-end consumer credit plan by the Truth in Lending Act apply, with the finance charge stated as the dollar amount of each charge and of the total charges for the period and for the calendar year to date on every periodic statement, and as the figures under section 2A-1 in every advertisement and account-opening disclosure. No annual percentage rate is stated for overdraft credit, and section 107 of that Act does not apply to it; the omission is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(b)** The figures under section 2A-1 for the class to which the account belongs are stated in every advertisement for the account and in the disclosure furnished when the account is opened, as Title II-A requires.\n\n**(c)** A debit card or other device that accesses overdraft credit is not a credit card for purposes of the Truth in Lending Act, and the provisions of that Act that apply only to a credit card account under an open-end consumer credit plan do not apply to overdraft credit; the omission is a determination of Congress. Section 161 of that Act applies to overdraft credit as to any other open-end consumer credit plan."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. Construction.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 OVERDRAFT IS CREDIT","body":"Nothing in this title limits the amount of any charge, requires any financial institution to offer or continue any service, or establishes any benchmark, safe-harbor amount, or rate for any charge; section 8-1 applies to this title."},{"anchor":"sec-4a-1","heading":"\u00a7 4A-1. Exclusion from consumer reports.","part":"TITLE IV-A \u2014 MEDICAL DEBT LEAVES THE CREDIT FILE","body":"**(a)** Section 603 of the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681a) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(bb) Medical debt information.** The term \"medical debt information\" means information about a debt, an account, or a balance arising from the receipt of medical services, products, or devices, including a debt owed to a health care provider, facility, or supplier, or to a person that has purchased the debt or collects it on another's behalf, whether the debt is paid, unpaid, settled, disputed, or placed for collection, and whether or not the information is restricted or reported using codes; and includes information about a debt under a credit card, loan, installment plan, or other financing used to pay for such services, products, or devices where the financing was arranged by, or offered through, the health care provider, facility, or supplier at or in connection with the provision of the services, products, or devices, and that provider, facility, or supplier receives any payment, fee, discount, or other consideration in connection with the financing or is an affiliate of the person extending it.\n\n**(b)** Section 605(a) of that Act (15 U.S.C. 1681c(a)) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(9) Medical debt information.**\n\n**(c)** Section 604(g)(1) of that Act (15 U.S.C. 1681b(g)(1)) is amended by striking \"; or\" at the end of subparagraph (B)(ii) and inserting a period, and by striking subparagraph (C), so that the paragraph ends as follows, with the struck text marked:\n\n> (ii) the consumer provides specific written consent for the furnishing of the report that describes in clear and conspicuous language the use for which the information will be furnished~~; or~~**.**\n>\n> ~~(C) the information to be furnished pertains solely to transactions, accounts, or balances relating to debts arising from the receipt of medical services, products, or devises, where such information, other than account status or amounts, is restricted or reported using codes that do not identify, or do not provide information sufficient to infer, the specific provider or the nature of such services, products, or devices, as provided in section 1681c(a)(6) of this title.~~","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV-A \u2014 MEDICAL DEBT LEAVES THE CREDIT FILE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4a-2","heading":"\u00a7 4A-2. Exclusion from credit decisions.","part":"TITLE IV-A \u2014 MEDICAL DEBT LEAVES THE CREDIT FILE","body":"Section 604(g)(2) of the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681b(g)(2)) is amended to read as follows, with new language marked as inserted:\n\n> (2) Limitation on creditors. Except as permitted pursuant to paragraph (3)(C) or regulations prescribed under paragraph (5)(A), a creditor shall not obtain or use medical information (other than medical information treated in the manner required under section 1681c(a)(6) of this title) pertaining to a consumer in connection with any determination of the consumer's eligibility, or continued eligibility, for credit. **A creditor shall not obtain or use medical debt information pertaining to a consumer, however treated, restricted, or coded, in connection with any such determination, and no regulation or order under paragraph (3)(C) or (5)(A) may permit it; except that a creditor that provided the medical services, products, or devices to which a debt relates may consider that debt in a determination concerning credit extended for those services, products, or devices.**"},{"anchor":"sec-4a-3","heading":"\u00a7 4A-3. Persons treated as consumer reporting agencies.","part":"TITLE IV-A \u2014 MEDICAL DEBT LEAVES THE CREDIT FILE","body":"For purposes of sections 604(g) and 605(a)(9) of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, as amended by this title, and of the enforcement of those provisions, a person that regularly assembles or evaluates medical debt information on consumers and furnishes it, for a fee, dues, or on a cooperative basis, for use in whole or in part in establishing a consumer's eligibility for credit, insurance, employment, or housing, shall be treated as a consumer reporting agency, and the information it furnishes shall be treated as a consumer report, whatever the person calls itself or the information. This section does not alter the definition in section 603(f) of that Act for any other purpose."},{"anchor":"sec-4a-4","heading":"\u00a7 4A-4. State law.","part":"TITLE IV-A \u2014 MEDICAL DEBT LEAVES THE CREDIT FILE","body":"Section 625 of the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681t) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(e) Greater State protection. Subsection (b) does not preempt\u2014**\n>\n> **(1) a law of a State that prohibits or restricts the inclusion of medical debt information in a consumer report, or its use in a determination of eligibility for credit, insurance, employment, or housing, to the extent the law affords a consumer greater protection than Title IV-A of the Honest Price Act.**"},{"anchor":"sec-4a-5","heading":"\u00a7 4A-5. Validation.","part":"TITLE IV-A \u2014 MEDICAL DEBT LEAVES THE CREDIT FILE","body":"**(a)** For each of the number of years set in the schedule beginning with the year in which section 4A-1 takes effect, the Bureau publishes, in the form section 2A-3(b) states, the rate of delinquency of 90 days or more within 24 months of origination on consumer credit accounts opened during each calendar quarter, by decile of the credit score used in the origination, for accounts opened in the eight quarters before that section took effect and in each quarter after. The publication identifies, by citation, each provision of this Act and of any other Federal law affecting credit scores that took effect during the period, as section 2A-3(d) provides for its publication.\n\n**(b)** The Bureau computes the rates from the de-identified data it holds or obtains from the consumer reporting agencies described in section 603(p) of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, and not from any analysis those agencies prepare. Each such agency furnishes to the Bureau, on request and within the period set in the schedule, de-identified account-level data sufficient to compute the rates, and the request is not a condition of the agency's duty to furnish it for a period in which no request is made by the date the schedule sets. The metric is that stated in subsection (a), and no regulation may substitute another."},{"anchor":"sec-4a-6","heading":"\u00a7 4A-6. Enforcement.","part":"TITLE IV-A \u2014 MEDICAL DEBT LEAVES THE CREDIT FILE","body":"The amendments made by this title are enforced under sections 616, 617, and 621 of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, and section 4A-3 is enforced as a provision of that Act. Section 7-2 does not apply to a violation of this title, as section 7-2(e) states."},{"anchor":"sec-4b-1","heading":"\u00a7 4B-1. Definitions.","part":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":"In this title:\n\n**(a) Scoring model.** \"Scoring model\" means any formula, statistical tool, algorithm, rule set, or modeling system, however developed and whatever it is called, that evaluates financial information about a consumer, and whose output is used, or is expected to be used, in whole or in part to approve, reject, or evaluate a consumer for, or to set the terms of, credit, insurance, employment, the rental or lease of a dwelling, any other goods or services, or any other purpose for which a consumer report may be furnished under section 604 of the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681b). Every component that evaluates financial information is part of the scoring model, including a rule, override, or cutoff applied to the output of another such component.\n\n**(b) Score.** \"Score\" means any output of a scoring model about a consumer, whatever its form or name, including a number, letter, grade, tier, band, category, color, label, rank, percentile, indicator, pass-or-fail result, recommendation, or decision to approve, deny, or condition an application or to set its terms; and includes each numerical value from which such an output is derived. The exclusions in section 609(f)(2)(A)(ii) of the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681g(f)(2)(A)(ii)) do not apply to this title.\n\n**(c) Scoring provider.** \"Scoring provider\" means a person that, in the ordinary course of business:\n\n**(1)** for consideration, furnishes a score about a consumer to another person;\n\n**(2)** develops, licenses, sells, or otherwise makes available a scoring model for use by another person;\n\n**(3)** evaluates a consumer with a scoring model and, on behalf of, under agreement with, or for the benefit of another person, makes, recommends, or communicates a decision about the consumer's eligibility for, or the terms of, any of the things described in subsection (a), whether the person acts as agent, contractor, platform, or assignee of the other person, or in its own name for a transaction that the other person funds, purchases, or takes by assignment; or\n\n**(4)** computes a score about a consumer with a scoring model, whether a model it developed itself or one another person developed, licensed, sold, or made available to it, including for its own decisions about the consumer.\n\nA person is not a scoring provider under paragraph (4) by reason only of applying, to a score that another scoring provider computed and discloses under section 4B-3, a cutoff that uses no other financial information; and a person that relies on the written statement of that other scoring provider that it complies with section 4B-3 is not a scoring provider on that account.\n\n**(d) Inputs.** \"Inputs\" means every item of information about a consumer that a scoring model used to compute a score, in the form the model used it, including each value the model derived from the consumer's information.\n\n**(e) Financial information.** \"Financial information\" means information bearing on a consumer's credit worthiness, credit standing, or credit capacity, and information about a consumer's borrowing, repayment, payment of bills, income, assets, deposits, spending, saving, or other financial behavior.\n\n**(f) Separation from other information.** Where a person evaluates a consumer on financial information together with other information, the scoring model is the part of the evaluation that assesses the financial information, and this title does not reach the part that weighs other information or the final decision that weighs both, if that decision uses no financial information other than the output of the scoring model. Where the person does not compute its assessment of the financial information separately from the other information, the whole model that evaluates the financial information is the scoring model.\n\n**(g) Consumer.** \"Consumer\" means any natural person about whom a score is computed, furnished, or acted on, and any natural person who applies for anything described in subsection (a), including an applicant for employment or for the rental or lease of a dwelling.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4b-2","heading":"\u00a7 4B-2. The formula is published.","part":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":"**(a)** Each scoring provider described in section 4B-1(c)(2), and each other scoring provider as to a scoring model for which no publication under this section exists, publishes for each scoring model, and for each version of it, the complete formula: every input; how each input is derived from the information about the consumer; every weight, coefficient, point value, threshold, segment, and rule; the function by which the inputs are combined; and each boundary by which a numerical value is mapped to a category, grade, recommendation, or decision. The formula is stated so that any person can compute, from a set of inputs, the score the model produces. Where the model cannot be so stated, the provider publishes the model itself in a form any person can run without charge, with the documentation needed to run it.\n\n**(b)** With the formula, the provider publishes the method by which the amounts under section 4B-3(c)(3) are computed, under which the amounts and a published base value add to the score.\n\n**(c)** The publication is free of charge, available online without registration, and continuous, and is made before the first use of the model or version after this title takes effect. A publication is kept available for the period set in the schedule after the version's last use.\n\n**(d)** No scoring provider furnishes, communicates, uses, or acts on a score computed with a scoring model, or a version of one, that is not published under this section. Publication by any person satisfies this section for every scoring provider as to that model and version.\n\n**(e)** If the duty to publish under subsection (a) is held invalid as to publication to the public, each scoring provider that subsection describes instead furnishes the complete formula, in the form subsection (a) states, free of charge, to the Bureau and to any consumer about whom a score was computed with the model, on request, and subsection (d) applies to a model not so furnished.\n\n**(f)** Nothing in this title withdraws or limits the remedy under section 1491 of title 28, United States Code, for any taking of property effected by this title, and that remedy is the exclusive remedy for such a taking."},{"anchor":"sec-4b-3","heading":"\u00a7 4B-3. Your statement.","part":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":"**(a) Every year.** At least once in each calendar year, each scoring provider described in section 4B-1(c)(1), (3), or (4) sends a statement under this section to each consumer about whom, in the preceding twelve months, it maintained a file from which it computes scores, or computed, furnished, or acted on a score.\n\n**(b) Models covered.** The statement covers each scoring model with which the provider computed a score about the consumer that was furnished, communicated, used, or acted on in the preceding twelve months, and, where there is none, the scoring model the provider used most often for all consumers during that period.\n\n**(c) Contents.** For each scoring model covered, the statement states:\n\n**(1)** the consumer's score computed from the information in the file on the date of the statement, or, where the provider holds no file, the most recent score it computed or acted on, with the inputs then used; where the model declines to score the consumer, the rule under which it declines and what information would satisfy it; and each score about the consumer furnished, communicated, used, or acted on in the preceding twelve months, with its date;\n\n**(2)** the range of possible scores and, where the output is a category, grade, recommendation, or decision, each such output and the range of numerical values from which it is derived;\n\n**(3)** a table listing every input, its value for the consumer, and the amount by which it raised or lowered the score, computed by the method published under section 4B-2(b) or, where none is published, by a method the statement states under which the amounts and the base value add to the score, with the base value, so that the amounts and the base value add to the score;\n\n**(4)** a table headed \"If you had paid off this share of what you owe, and nothing else about your finances were different, your score would be,\" stating the score the model would produce for each of 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90, and 100 percent, computed by reducing every balance owed on every account in the file by that percentage, closing no account, and leaving every other input unchanged, or, where the provider holds no account-level information, by reducing each input that is an amount owed by that percentage and recomputing each input derived from it;\n\n**(5)** where the formula is published under section 4B-2, or that it is furnished to the Bureau under section 4B-2(e);\n\n**(6)** the toll-free telephone number and the website under subsection (f), in words stating that free and low-cost credit counseling is available there; and\n\n**(7)** how the consumer may view the statement online at any time, and opt out of or back into mailed yearly statements, free of charge, and how the consumer may dispute an input.\n\n**(d) Form.** The statement is written in plain language. Every score the statement covers, and the tables under subsection (c)(3) and (c)(4) for the scoring model whose scores about the consumer were furnished, used, or acted on most often in the preceding twelve months, appear on its first page or first screen, and the tables for each other model follow. No advertisement, offer, or solicitation appears in the statement, on the website under subsection (g) where the statement is viewed, or in the process by which a consumer requests, views, or opts out of it. Within the period set in the schedule after enactment, the Bureau publishes a model statement, and a statement in the form of the model satisfies the first sentence of this subsection. The duty under this section does not depend on the model.\n\n**(e) Yearly delivery.** The statement under subsection (a) is sent by mail to the address the consumer last confirmed to the provider, or, where there is none, to the most recent address the provider holds that is not subject to a fraud alert or block under section 605A or 605B of the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681c-1, 1681c-2). Where the provider holds no address, the person on whose behalf it acted supplies, on request, the address the consumer gave in the application. The statement may be enclosed, as a separate document, in a mailing the provider otherwise sends the consumer. A consumer may at any time opt out of mailed yearly statements, and then receives the yearly statement by email at the address the consumer gives; the consumer may opt back in at any time. An opt-out is made only by an affirmative act of the consumer, is never preselected or a condition of anything, and may be made and revoked by any means the provider uses to communicate with consumers, including online and by a toll-free telephone number.\n\n**(f) Counseling.** Within the period set in the schedule after enactment, the Bureau establishes and maintains a toll-free telephone number and a website that refer a consumer, by the consumer's State, to nonprofit budget and credit counseling agencies approved under section 111 of title 11, United States Code, and to housing counseling agencies certified under section 106 of the Housing and Urban Development Act of 1968 (12 U.S.C. 1701x). Where the number or website is not in operation on the date of the statement, the statement gives in their place where the lists maintained under section 111(a) of title 11, United States Code, may be consulted.\n\n**(g) Online at any time.** Each scoring provider described in subsection (a) maintains a website on which a consumer may, at any time, view the statement under subsection (c), computed from the information the provider holds about the consumer at the moment the consumer views it, or, where the provider holds no file, as subsection (c)(1) provides. A consumer may also request a mailed statement by telephone or by mail, and the provider honors at least one such request from each consumer in each calendar year, in addition to the statement under subsection (a). A scoring provider whose scoring model consists only of values stated on the application under section 4B-3B need not maintain the website, and instead sends the statement with its decision, and a copy on request.\n\n**(h) No charge.** No charge is imposed for any statement, or for access to the website, under this section, and neither is conditioned on the purchase of, enrollment in, or agreement to anything. The provider requires no identification for any of them beyond what is reasonably necessary to verify that the requester is the consumer, and a consumer reporting agency requires no more than it requires for a disclosure under section 609 of the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681g). The provider furnishes no statement, and no access to the website, to a requester not so verified."},{"anchor":"sec-4b-3a","heading":"\u00a7 4B-3A. Closures.","part":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":"**(a)** A scoring provider that furnishes, communicates, or acts on a grade, tier, recommendation, decision, or other output in place of a number has every duty under this title as to that output and as to each numerical value from which it is derived, and, where no numerical value exists, as to the output itself, stated for each row of the table under section 4B-3(c)(4).\n\n**(b)** A person described in section 4B-1(c)(3) is a scoring provider whether its output is called a score, a screening result, a recommendation, an approval, a denial, or the terms offered, and whether or not it furnishes any score to another person.\n\n**(c)** Nothing in this title requires a scoring provider to maintain a file, compute a score, or furnish a score it does not otherwise compute or furnish, other than the score a statement under section 4B-3 requires."},{"anchor":"sec-4b-3b","heading":"\u00a7 4B-3B. Cutoffs stated on the application.","part":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":"**(a)** A person that rejects an application for anything described in section 4B-1(a), or conditions it on a cosigner, guarantor, or additional deposit, or on a payment, bond, insurance, or fee required in place of any of them, in whole or in part because a score or other financial information about the applicant falls above or below a stated value, states on the application, before the consumer submits it or pays any application fee, each such value, stated as the value below which it always so rejects or conditions and the value at or above which it never does so on that ground, the score or financial information to which it applies, the name and version of the scoring model that produces the score, and the effect of falling short of it. Where another person takes the application on the person's behalf, including a dealer, broker, or platform, the person gives that other person each such value, and that other person states it on the application.\n\n**(a-1)** A rejection or condition made after the person receives a score below a boundary that a scoring provider designates for rejection or for further review is made on the ground of that boundary for purposes of this section, whoever makes it.\n\n**(a-2)** \"Application fee\" means any amount paid, to the person or to another at its direction, to have an application considered.\n\n**(b)** A person shall not reject or condition an application on the ground of a value it did not state on the application under subsection (a), or of a value less favorable to the applicant than the one it stated, and refunds any application fee paid by a consumer whose application it rejects or conditions on such a ground.\n\n**(c)** This section applies to every person that receives applications for anything described in section 4B-1(a), whether or not it is a scoring provider, and to a value applied on its behalf by an agent, contractor, or decision service. A person that applies a value under this section is a covered person for purposes of Titles VII and VII-A as to this section.\n\n**(d)** A covered person that files under section 2A-3(a) files with it each value it stated under this section for the class during the standard period, and the Bureau publishes the values beside the rates under section 2A-4(c)."},{"anchor":"sec-4b-4","heading":"\u00a7 4B-4. Relation to the Fair Credit Reporting Act.","part":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":"**(a)** Section 609(f)(8) of the Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C. 1681g(f)(8)) is amended to read as follows, with new language marked as inserted:\n\n> (8) Fair and reasonable fee. A consumer reporting agency may charge a fair and reasonable fee, as determined by the Bureau, for providing the information required under this subsection**, except that no fee may be charged for any score or statement that Title IV-B of the Honest Price Act requires**.\n\n**(b)** Section 625(e) of that Act, as added by section 4A-4, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(2) a law of a State requiring the disclosure of a score, of a scoring model, or of the inputs to a score, as those terms are defined in section 4B-1 of the Honest Price Act, or the statement on an application of a value of the kind described in section 4B-3B of that Act, to the extent the law affords a consumer greater protection than Title IV-B of that Act.**\n\n**(c)** This title is cumulative of every Federal law requiring the disclosure of a score or of the reasons for an action, including sections 609(f), 609(g), and 615 of that Act (15 U.S.C. 1681g(f), 1681g(g), 1681m) and section 701(d) of the Equal Credit Opportunity Act (15 U.S.C. 1691(d)), and no disclosure under any of them satisfies this title."},{"anchor":"sec-4b-5","heading":"\u00a7 4B-5. Policies of insurance.","part":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":"**(a)** This title specifically relates to the business of insurance as applied to a score used in connection with insurance, and applies to such a score notwithstanding section 2(b) of the Act of March 9, 1945 (15 U.S.C. 1012(b)).\n\n**(b)** If subsection (a) is held invalid or inapplicable as to any State law, this title does not invalidate, impair, or supersede that State law to the extent it regulates the business of insurance, and applies to a score used in connection with insurance to the extent the law of the State does not govern its disclosure. The invalidity of subsection (a) does not affect subsection (b) or any other provision of this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-4b-6","heading":"\u00a7 4B-6. Enforcement.","part":"TITLE IV-B \u2014 YOUR SCORE, WITH ITS ARITHMETIC","body":"**(a)** A violation of this title is a violation of this Act, and a scoring provider is a covered person for purposes of Titles VII and VII-A as to this title. For purposes of Titles VII and VII-A and section 7-6 as to this title, a consumer as section 4B-1(g) defines the term is a consumer, and an application fee is a charge.\n\n**(b)** For purposes of section 7-2(a), a consumer is aggrieved by a violation of this title where the scoring provider maintained a file on the consumer, or computed, furnished, or acted on a score about the consumer, and failed to send a statement section 4B-3 requires or to make it available online as section 4B-3(g) requires, sent or displayed one that misstates or omits what section 4B-3(c) requires, charged for or conditioned a statement, or computed, furnished, or acted on a score about the consumer with a scoring model not published under section 4B-2; and a consumer is aggrieved by a violation of section 4B-3B where the consumer applied to the person and the application was rejected or conditioned on the ground of a value not stated on it, or the consumer paid an application fee the section requires refunded. The money charged or paid is the injury. Where a statement was withheld or misstated, or a score was computed with a model not published, the injury is the consumer's loss of the information with which to dispute an input, to correct an error before a score is furnished to a third party, or to decide whether and where to apply; and a consumer who alleges that a score about the consumer was furnished, used, or acted on while that information was withheld or misstated alleges that loss.\n\n**(c)** A scoring provider keeps, for the period set in the schedule under section 7-5, a record of each statement sent and each request received under section 4B-3, sufficient to identify the consumer, the date, the scoring models covered, and the scores and inputs stated or acted on; and a record of each opt-out and opt-in under section 4B-3(e).\n\n**(d)** Section 4B-4(a) and (b) are enforced as provisions of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, in addition to this section."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Terms governing forum, remedy, and procedure.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE SUBSTITUTION MENU CLOSED","body":"**(a)** A consumer agreement may govern the forum, remedy, or procedure for a consumer dispute only by:\n\n**(1)** a post-dispute agreement that satisfies section 1-2; or\n\n**(2)** a term that applies equally to the consumer and the covered person, and that does not reduce, limit, shorten, waive, or condition any forum, remedy, procedure, or period available to the consumer under any law.\n\n**(b)** Any other term of a consumer agreement governing the forum, remedy, or procedure for a consumer dispute, regardless of form, title, or characterization, is void. Without limiting subsection (a), the following are void:\n\n**(1)** a predispute waiver of trial by jury;\n\n**(2)** a term requiring a consumer dispute to be brought or heard outside the State in which the consumer resides or the agreement was entered or performed;\n\n**(3)** a term selecting the law of a jurisdiction other than the consumer's State of residence, to the extent it reduces a protection the consumer would otherwise have;\n\n**(4)** a term limiting the damages or other relief available under any statute, or waiving punitive, statutory, or consequential damages a statute provides;\n\n**(5)** a term shortening the period within which a consumer dispute may be brought below the period a statute provides;\n\n**(6)** a term requiring the consumer to pay the covered person's attorney's fees or costs, unless the same term entitles the consumer to recover fees and costs on the same conditions;\n\n**(7)** a term waiving the right to seek public injunctive relief, or relief on behalf of persons other than the consumer, where a statute provides it;\n\n**(8)** a term permitting the covered person to modify the agreement unilaterally as to a consumer dispute that has already arisen;\n\n**(9)** a term requiring the consumer to notify the covered person, to exhaust an internal or administrative process, or to wait a period, before asserting a claim, other than the demand under section 7A-1; and\n\n**(10)** a term waiving, or purporting to waive, any right under this Act.\n\n**(c)** A term void under section 45b of title 15, United States Code, is void under this section as well.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 THE SUBSTITUTION MENU CLOSED","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Effect.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE SUBSTITUTION MENU CLOSED","body":"**(a)** A term void under section 5-1 is void without any determination by any officer, agency, or court, is severed from the consumer agreement, and the remainder of the agreement stands notwithstanding any term to the contrary.\n\n**(b)** A covered person violates this Act by invoking, or seeking to enforce, a term void under section 5-1, or by including such a term in a consumer agreement entered, renewed, or modified more than the period set in the schedule after the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(c)** This title applies to an agreement entered before, on, or after the date of enactment of this Act, as to a consumer dispute arising on or after that date."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Prohibited interface practices.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERFACE CLOSURES","body":"**(a)** In offering, selling, or administering a consumer agreement, a covered person shall not:\n\n**(1)** present an optional addition, or any charge, as preselected, selected by default, or requiring an act by the consumer to decline;\n\n**(2)** require more steps, more time, more information, or a different channel to decline an offer, reject an addition, cancel an agreement, or withdraw consent than the covered person required to accept, enroll, purchase, or give consent;\n\n**(3)** present the option to decline, cancel, or reject with wording, size, color, placement, or timing less prominent than the option to accept, or in wording that requires the consumer to negate a negative to decline;\n\n**(4)** interpose, in a flow by which the consumer cancels, declines, or rejects, an offer or question that the consumer must answer more than once, or a step that has no function other than delay, and a step has no function other than delay where the cancellation, declination, or rejection could be completed without it and it collects no information necessary to complete it;\n\n**(5)** state that an offer, price, or availability is limited in time or quantity when it is not; or\n\n**(6)** disclose a mandatory charge only after the consumer has entered payment information.\n\n**(b)** This section states the practices it prohibits and no other, and the omission of a practice is a determination of Congress; but nothing in this section limits any other law, and a practice this section does not reach may be unfair or deceptive under section 5 of the Federal Trade Commission Act or under State law.\n\n**(c)** A covered person violates this Act by each instance of a practice prohibited by subsection (a).","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERFACE CLOSURES","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. Publication of judgments.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERFACE CLOSURES","body":"**(a)** The Commission publishes, in one place and in the form section 2A-3(b) states, every final judgment of a court finding a violation of this title, identifying the covered person, the practice, and the court, within the period set in the schedule after the judgment becomes final.\n\n**(b)** A settlement, consent order, or other resolution not embodied in a judgment finding a violation is published only in aggregate by number and by practice, without identifying any party, and no inference of liability arises from a resolution or its publication."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. No agency action is required.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT THAT WAITS ON NO ONE","body":"**(a)** Every duty, entitlement, and consequence in this Act operates without any rulemaking, guidance, designation, determination, or other action by any agency or officer of the United States. A term this Act makes void is void without any such action and without any judgment.\n\n**(b)** No agency is authorized to exempt any person from this Act, to add a coverage test, to narrow a definition in this Act, to permit a practice this Act prohibits, or to define or interpret by regulation section 1-1, 1-2, 2-1, 2A-2, 4-2, 4B-1, 4B-3, 5-1, 6-1, or 7A-6.\n\n**(c)** Section 2A-3(c), (e), and (f) apply to every publication duty under this Act, including sections 4A-5, 4B-3(d) and (f), 6-2, 7-2(m), 7A-1(a), and 7A-4, and to the receipt of every filing this Act requires.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT THAT WAITS ON NO ONE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Private action.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT THAT WAITS ON NO ONE","body":"**(a)** A consumer aggrieved by a violation of this Act may bring a civil action against the covered person, and against any affiliate that participated in the violation. A consumer is aggrieved by a violation where the consumer entered, was charged under, or paid under the consumer agreement, display, or practice in which the violation occurred, or where the consumer's election, consent, demand, or cancellation was disregarded; and the money so paid or charged, and the disregarded act, are the injury. A consumer who is not aggrieved within this subsection may proceed in a State court as State law provides.\n\n**(b)** The court shall award the greater of the consumer's actual damages or the statutory amount set in the schedule for each violation; three times that amount where the violation was willful or where the covered person refused a demand under section 7A-1 in bad faith with reason to know of the violation; and such injunctive and declaratory relief as is necessary and proper, including relief on behalf of persons other than the consumer.\n\n**(c)** An action under this section may be maintained as a class action, and a consumer who has not made a demand under section 7A-1 may bring or join one.\n\n**(c-1)** In a class action, the total of the statutory amounts recoverable under subsection (b) for violations of section 2-2, of section 6-1(a)(3) or (4), or of section 4B-3(d) as to the placement, order, or plain language of a statement, other than willful violations, shall not exceed the lesser of the amount set in the schedule or one percent of the net worth of the covered person. No limit under this subsection applies to actual damages; to a willful violation; or to any violation by which a charge was collected, a void term was invoked, an election, consent, demand, or cancellation was disregarded, or a refund was withheld, and a violation of section 2-2 or 6-1(a)(3) or (4) in the course of which a charge was collected is a violation by which the charge was collected.\n\n**(d)** A prevailing consumer recovers reasonable attorney's fees and costs. A covered person recovers fees and costs only where the court finds that the action was brought or maintained in bad faith or was frivolous, and section 7A-3(c) governs fees after a tender.\n\n**(e)** This section does not apply to a violation of Title IV-A, which is enforced as that title states.\n\n**(f)** An action under this section may be brought within the period set in the schedule after the later of the violation or the consumer's discovery of it.\n\n**(g)** An action under this Act may be brought in a district court of the United States or in any State court of competent jurisdiction. Such an action brought in a State court may not be removed to a district court of the United States, notwithstanding section 1441 or 1453 of title 28, United States Code. A State court shall not decline to hear such an action on the ground that it arises under Federal law, nor dismiss it on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States.\n\n**(h)** Neither exhaustion of any administrative remedy, nor notice to any officer or agency, nor a determination by any agency, is a condition of an action under this section. A demand under section 7A-1 is not a condition of an action under this section.\n\n**(i)** Nothing in this Act authorizes a person to sue as an assignee, in whole or in part, of a claim of the United States.\n\n**(j)** A violation is established by the conduct this Act describes. No showing of intent, knowledge, reliance, actual deception, or actual injury beyond the violation is required, except where this Act uses the word \"willful\" or \"bad faith,\" and no other causal standard applies. The defenses to an action under this Act are those this Act states and no other; the common law supplies none, and the omission of a defense is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(k)** A claim under this Act is stated by alleging the conduct constituting the violation. Knowledge, intent, and motive may be pleaded generally.\n\n**(l)** A term of a consumer agreement selecting the forum or the law of another jurisdiction is void to the extent it would apply to an action under this Act.\n\n**(m)** The statutory amount set in the schedule is adjusted on the first day of each calendar year by the change in the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers over the preceding year, rounded up to the nearest dollar, and may be reduced only at a review occurring no more often than the period set in the schedule, on a published statement of reasons. The Bureau publishes the adjusted amount before each calendar year begins; the adjustment operates whether or not it is published."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Enforcement by a State.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT THAT WAITS ON NO ONE","body":"The attorney general of a State may bring a civil action for the relief available under section 7-2, and for civil penalties in the amount that section 7-2(b) provides for each violation, on behalf of the residents of that State, in any court described in section 7-2(g). This section supplements the action under section 7-2 and is not a precondition of it, is not conditioned on notice to any Federal agency, and is not barred by a pending action of the United States concerning the same conduct."},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Federal enforcement.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT THAT WAITS ON NO ONE","body":"**(a)** A violation of this Act shall be treated as a violation of a rule under section 18 of the Federal Trade Commission Act (15 U.S.C. 57a) regarding unfair or deceptive acts or practices, and the Commission shall enforce this Act in the same manner, by the same means, and with the same jurisdiction, powers, and duties as though all applicable terms and provisions of the Federal Trade Commission Act were incorporated into and made a part of this Act.\n\n**(b)** This Act shall be treated as an enumerated consumer law for purposes of the Consumer Financial Protection Act of 2010, and a violation of this Act by a covered person subject to that Act shall be treated as a violation of a Federal consumer financial law.\n\n**(c)** The Commission's authority under subsection (a) extends to every covered person notwithstanding section 5(a)(2) of the Federal Trade Commission Act, and is concurrent with the Bureau's under subsection (b). A violation of this Act by an air carrier or foreign air carrier, or by a ticket agent, is an unfair or deceptive practice for purposes of section 41712 of title 49, United States Code, and the Secretary of Transportation enforces it under that section in addition to the Commission.\n\n**(d)** No subsection of this section is a precondition of, limits, or displaces any action under section 7-2 or 7-3."},{"anchor":"sec-7-5","heading":"\u00a7 7-5. Records.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT THAT WAITS ON NO ONE","body":"A covered person keeps, for the period set in the schedule, a record of every charge posted to each account, sufficient to identify the account, the consumer, the amount, the date, and the term or product under which the charge was imposed, and of every demand, tender, and refund under Title VII-A."},{"anchor":"sec-7-6","heading":"\u00a7 7-6. Tax treatment and effect on benefits.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT THAT WAITS ON NO ONE","body":"**(a)** A refund of a charge under this Act, including under section 7A-2 or 7A-6, is a return of the amount paid and not income to the consumer, and no information return is required for it.\n\n**(b)** Gross income does not include the statutory amount received by a consumer under section 7A-2 or 7A-3. Section 104(a) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by inserting after paragraph (1) the following: \"**(1C) amounts received under section 7A-2 or 7A-3 of the Honest Price Act;**\". No information return is required for such an amount.\n\n**(c)** Section 62(e) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**(19) Any provision of the Honest Price Act.**\"\n\n**(d)** An amount received by a consumer under this Act shall not be taken into account as income or as a resource in determining eligibility for, or the amount of, any Federal or federally assisted benefit conditioned on need.\n\n**(e)** No deduction is allowed under chapter 1 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 for the amount by which a payment under, or in settlement or satisfaction of a claim under, section 7-2(b), 7-3, 7A-2, or 7A-3 exceeds the sum of the charge refunded and the consumer's actual damages, or for any amount by which an award is trebled. A civil penalty under section 7-3 or 7-4 is an amount described in section 162(f)(1) of that Code."},{"anchor":"sec-7a-1","heading":"\u00a7 7A-1. Demand.","part":"TITLE VII-A \u2014 SMALL CLAIMS","body":"**(a)** A consumer may deliver to a covered person a written demand that states that it is made under this Act and identifies the consumer; the account or transaction; and the charge, term, or practice complained of. No form is required, and the consumer need not identify any provision of this Act. A demand may be delivered by any means the covered person accepts communications from consumers, including any complaint channel it maintains and the Bureau's complaint process. Within the period set in the schedule after enactment, the Bureau publishes a model demand that fits on one side of one letter-size page, that asks for nothing this subsection does not require, and that a consumer can complete online or on paper and deliver online, by mail, or in person; and the Bureau keeps the model available online and, on request, by mail. A written demand that meets this subsection is effective whether or not the model is used or exists.\n\n**(b)** A demand concerning a charge identifies a charge actually posted to the demander's own account, or a transaction to which the demander was a party, and a demand that does not is not a demand under this title.\n\n**(c)** A demand, and the right to the scheduled amount, may not be sold, assigned, or transferred before the amount is paid, and any purported assignment is void.\n\n**(d)** A demand made with knowledge that it is false is not payable, and any amount paid on it is recoverable. No other consequence attaches to an unsuccessful demand made in good faith.\n\n**(e)** The scheduled amount for a demand is the amount of the charge complained of, if any, plus the statutory amount set in the schedule under section 7-2(b).","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VII-A \u2014 SMALL CLAIMS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7a-2","heading":"\u00a7 7A-2. Tender.","part":"TITLE VII-A \u2014 SMALL CLAIMS","body":"**(a)** Within the period set in the schedule after receiving a demand, the covered person may tender the scheduled amount to the consumer, by payment in the original form of payment or by any means the consumer accepts. A tender conditioned on any release, agreement, or act by the consumer other than acceptance of the payment is not a tender under this title.\n\n**(b)** A tender of the full scheduled amount, whether or not accepted, is a complete satisfaction of the consumer's claim for statutory damages and fees for that violation as to that consumer. Where the consumer rejects the tender and brings an action for the same violation, the consumer's recovery is limited to the scheduled amount and the consumer's actual damages beyond it, and section 7A-3(c) governs fees.\n\n**(c)** A tender is not an admission of a violation, is inadmissible as evidence of one, and may be introduced by the covered person only to establish the tender."},{"anchor":"sec-7a-3","heading":"\u00a7 7A-3. Refusal or silence.","part":"TITLE VII-A \u2014 SMALL CLAIMS","body":"**(a)** Where the covered person has not tendered the full scheduled amount within the period set in the schedule, the scheduled amount is a liquidated sum owed to the consumer, recoverable in any court, including a court or division for small claims, together with costs and the consumer's own reasonable expenses of recovery.\n\n**(b)** Where the court finds that the refusal was made in bad faith with reason to know of the violation, the court awards three times the statutory amount, as section 7-2(b) provides.\n\n**(c)** A consumer who rejects a tender that the court finds was of the full scheduled amount recovers no attorney's fees or costs incurred after the tender.\n\n**(d)** A demand that identifies a charge on an open-end credit account or an electronic fund transfer may be made as a notice under section 161 of the Truth in Lending Act (15 U.S.C. 1666) or section 908 of the Electronic Fund Transfer Act (15 U.S.C. 1693f), as section 7A-5 provides, and the remedies of those sections are cumulative of this title."},{"anchor":"sec-7a-4","heading":"\u00a7 7A-4. Counts published.","part":"TITLE VII-A \u2014 SMALL CLAIMS","body":"With each filing under section 2A-3(a), a covered person that files under that section states, for each functional product class, the number of demands received, the number tendered in full, the number refused or not answered, and the number of refunds made under section 7A-6, during the standard period; and the Bureau publishes each statement in the table under section 2A-3(b) for that class, in the same form, subject to section 2A-3(c) through (f)."},{"anchor":"sec-7a-5","heading":"\u00a7 7A-5. Existing procedures.","part":"TITLE VII-A \u2014 SMALL CLAIMS","body":"**(a)** Section 161(b) of the Truth in Lending Act (15 U.S.C. 1666(b)) is amended by inserting after paragraph (6) the following:\n\n> **(6A) A charge imposed under a term void under the Honest Price Act, or in violation of that Act.**\n\n**(b)** Section 908(f) of the Electronic Fund Transfer Act (15 U.S.C. 1693f(f)) is amended by striking \"or\" at the end of paragraph (6) and by inserting after paragraph (6) the following:\n\n> **(6A) a charge imposed under a term void under the Honest Price Act, or in violation of that Act; or**\n\n**(c)** A creditor or financial institution that receives a notice under a paragraph added by this section complies with the section under which it was received, and the notice is also a demand under section 7A-1 for purposes of this title."},{"anchor":"sec-7a-6","heading":"\u00a7 7A-6. Refund to every account.","part":"TITLE VII-A \u2014 SMALL CLAIMS","body":"**(a) The trigger.** This section applies when a term of a consumer agreement is found void under this Act, or a charge is found to have been imposed in violation of it, by:\n\n**(1)** a final judgment of a court of record in a contested action under section 7-2 or 7-3, or in an action by the Commission or the Bureau;\n\n**(2)** a consent order, settlement, or other resolution entered with the Commission, the Bureau, or the attorney general of a State; or\n\n**(3)** the covered person's written admission.\n\nA judgment entered by default, or in a court or division for small claims, is not a trigger under this subsection, and no event other than those this subsection states is a trigger. An action is contested where the covered person appeared and the question whether the term is void or the charge unlawful was litigated. A term is substantially similar to a term found void where it produces the same charge, or has the same effect on the consumer, in the same circumstances, whatever its wording.\n\n**(b) The duty.** Within the period set in the schedule after a trigger under subsection (a) becomes final, the covered person refunds, to every consumer whose account records under section 7-5 show a charge imposed under that term, or under any substantially similar term used by the covered person or any affiliate, or in the same violation, during the period set in the schedule under section 7-2(f) before the trigger, the amount of the charge, in the original form of payment or, where that form is unavailable, by any means the consumer accepts, without any request by the consumer and without retaining any fee. A refund under this subsection is due whatever name, product, tier, or channel the charge was imposed under.\n\n**(c) Accounts closed or unreachable.** For an account closed or a consumer not reachable at the last address or payment instrument the records show, the covered person holds the refund for the period set in the schedule and then delivers it under the unclaimed-property law of the State of the consumer's last known address. No refund under this section reverts to the covered person.\n\n**(d) Safe harbor on demand.** A covered person that, within the tender period under section 7A-2 after a demand under section 7A-1, refunds under subsection (b) every account the demand's charge, term, or practice reached, ceases the practice, and files with the Bureau a statement of the number of accounts refunded and the amount, is not liable under section 7-2(b) for statutory damages for that charge, term, or practice as to any consumer, and no class may be maintained under section 7-2(c) as to it; a consumer's actual damages beyond the refund remain recoverable. A refund and statement under this subsection is not an admission of a violation and is inadmissible as evidence of one, except to establish the refund.\n\n**(e) Records.** The refund runs to every account identifiable from the records section 7-5 requires or from any record the covered person in fact keeps, and the absence of a record the covered person was required to keep is not a defense.\n\n**(f) Relation to other relief.** A refund under this section is credited against any restitution or damages otherwise awarded for the same charge, and does not limit any other relief."},{"anchor":"sec-8-1","heading":"\u00a7 8-1. Rules of construction.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Nothing in this Act establishes, limits, or approves any price, rate, or fee a covered person may charge a consumer, and no provision of this Act may be applied to set, cap, review, or approve the amount of any price, rate, fee, or charge. A figure published under Title II-A is information and not a limit.\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this Act requires any covered person to offer, continue, or discontinue any product, service, or feature, and nothing in this Act bans any product.\n\n**(c)** The omission from this Act of any regulation of a price, rate, fee, or product is a determination of Congress and not a gap, and no court or agency shall supply one.\n\n**(d)** This Act does not limit any right or protection arising under any other Federal, State, local, or Tribal law, and does not preempt a law affording greater protection to a consumer. No other provision of law is an exclusive remedy for, channels, or precludes a claim under this Act.\n\n**(e)** Nothing in this Act dictates the content of Tribal law. A Tribal government joins the provisions of this Act as section 0-3(p) provides, without any election, and is bound only as to a function voluntarily assumed.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-8-2","heading":"\u00a7 8-2. Severability and anti-aggregation.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"If any provision of this Act, or any application of a provision, is held invalid, every other provision and application stands. No court shall treat the provisions of this Act, or of this Act together with any other Act, as an integrated scheme whose parts stand or fall together. In particular, the invalidity of section 2-2 does not affect section 2-1 or any other provision; the invalidity of any provision of Title II-A does not affect any provision of Title II or Title IV; the invalidity of section 1-4(a) does not affect section 1-4(b) or (c) or any other provision; the invalidity of section 7A-6 does not affect any other provision of Title VII-A; the invalidity of section 4B-2 does not affect section 4B-3 or 4B-3A, and the invalidity of any paragraph of section 4B-3(c) does not affect any other paragraph; and the invalidity of any application of Title I to an agreement entered before the date of enactment does not affect its application to any agreement entered after."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3","heading":"\u00a7 8-3. Effective dates.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Titles I, V, and VII, and section 8-1, take effect on enactment. Title II and Title VI take effect 180 days after enactment. Title II-A takes effect on enactment as to section 2A-3 and section 2A-6, and the first standard period under section 2A-2(e) ends on the last day of the fourth full calendar quarter after enactment; section 2A-1 applies from the first filing under section 2A-3. Title III takes effect 180 days after enactment. Title IV takes effect on the first day of the second calendar quarter beginning after the first filing deadline under section 2A-3(a), and not earlier than one year after enactment. Title IV-A takes effect 180 days after enactment. Title IV-B takes effect one year after enactment, except that the Bureau's duties under section 4B-3(d) and (f) take effect on enactment. Title VII-A takes effect 180 days after enactment, and section 7A-6 applies to a trigger occurring after that date; but the Bureau's duty to publish the model demand under section 7A-1(a) takes effect on enactment."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4","heading":"\u00a7 8-4. Resources and continuity of operations.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"There is appropriated to the Bureau, for each fiscal year, the amount set in the schedule, without fiscal year limitation, for the publication duties of sections 2A-3, 4A-5, 4B-3(d) and (f), 6-2, and 7A-4, and the appropriation is not subject to apportionment delay or programmatic withholding. Those publication duties, and the receipt of filings under this Act, continue during any lapse in appropriations. **The officers and employees necessary to perform those duties shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations, identified as such in the published shutdown plan of the agency concerned.** No other function under this Act requires an appropriation."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE.","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 1-2(a)(3) period to consider a post-dispute agreement; revocation period | 21 days; 7 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 2A-1(e) minimum number of accounts below which the count is stated | 100 accounts | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 2A-2(b) functional product classes | deposit and transaction accounts; credit cards; other consumer loans; wireless service; broadband and video service; subscriptions and memberships; lease-purchase and rent-to-own agreements | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 2A-2(e) standard period; computation period after it ends | the calendar year; 60 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 2A-3(a) filing period after the standard period ends | 60 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 2A-3(b), (e) publication period after the filing deadline | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 2A-4(a) unpaid-balance period before closure is permitted | 60 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 2A-4(c) advertising media in which the rates accompany the figures | print, online, and mailed advertising | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 3-1, ROSCA \u00a7 4(a)(4) notice before the first post-trial charge; \u00a7 4(b)(4) confirmation of cancellation; \u00a7 4(d) notice before a change | 7 days; 3 business days; 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 4-2(a) courtesy-program limits per account per 12 months: number of charges; aggregate | 3 charges; $30 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 4-3(c) period to obtain consent on existing accounts | 12 months | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 5-2(b) period after enactment before inclusion of a void term is itself a violation | 180 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 6-2(a) period to publish a judgment | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 7-2(b) statutory amount per violation | $500 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14A | \u00a7 7-2(c-1) class limit on statutory amounts for presentation and interface-manner violations | $1,000,000 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-15 | \u00a7 7-2(f) limitation period | 3 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-16 | \u00a7 7-2(m) minimum interval between reviews that may reduce the statutory amount | 5 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-17 | \u00a7 7-5 record-retention period | the limitation period plus 1 year | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-18 | \u00a7 7A-2(a), \u00a7 7A-3(a) tender period | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-19 | \u00a7 4A-5(b) period for a consumer reporting agency to furnish data; date by which data is furnished absent a request | 60 days; March 1 of each year | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-20 | \u00a7 7A-6(b) period to refund every account after the trigger becomes final | 90 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-21 | \u00a7 7A-6(c) period to hold an undeliverable refund | 1 year | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-22 | \u00a7 4A-5(a) number of validation years | 5 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-23 | \u00a7 8-4 annual appropriation for publication duties | $20,000,000 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-24 | \u00a7 7A-1(a) period after enactment to publish the model demand | 120 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-25 | \u00a7 4B-2(c) period a published formula stays available after the version's last use | 7 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-26 | \u00a7 4B-3(d) period after enactment to publish the model statement; \u00a7 4B-3(f) period after enactment to establish the counseling number and website | 180 days; 180 days | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-19"}}}};
